9.2 Decreasing continuity and symmetry A bounded plurisubharmonic function need not be continuous (see the example after proposition 9.2.7 ), so in general it is not a uniform limit of continuous functions; it is, however, the limit of the decreasing sequence of its smooth regularizations. The main result of this section, theorem 9.2.5 , states that the products are continuous along decreasing sequences and symmetric in their factors. Upper semicontinuity bounds every weak limit of weighted products from above (lemma 9.2.2 ). To prove equality, we first modify all functions so that they coincide with one fixed smooth function near the boundary of a ball, and then compare total masses by an integration by parts (lemma 9.2.1 ). As applications, we prove that the products are local on sets of the form $\{ u>v\} $ (proposition 9.2.7 ) and derive the comparison principle (theorem 9.2.8 ). We use the real operator $\mathrm{d}^{\mathrm c}$ of ( 7.6 ), for which $\mathrm{d}\mathrm{d}^{\mathrm c}=\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}$. If $a$ and $b$ are smooth real functions, the $(1,1)$-part of $\mathrm{d}a\wedge \mathrm{d}^{\mathrm c}b$ is $ rac{\mathrm{i}}{4\pi }(\partial a\wedge  ar\partial b+\partial b\wedge  ar\partial a)$, which is symmetric in $a$ and $b$. Only this part survives multiplication by a current $R$ of bidegree $(n-1,n-1)$, so \[  \mathrm{d}a\wedge \mathrm{d}^{\mathrm c}b\wedge R=\mathrm{d}b\wedge \mathrm{d}^{\mathrm c}a\wedge R.  \] Moreover, the $(2,0)$- and $(0,2)$-parts of $\mathrm{d}a\wedge \mathrm{d}^{\mathrm c}a$ are multiples of $\partial a\wedge \partial a=0$ and $ ar\partial a\wedge  ar\partial a=0$, so 9.7  egin{equation} \label{eq:bt-gradient-form} \mathrm{d}a\wedge \mathrm{d}^{\mathrm c}a= rac{\mathrm{i}}{2\pi }\partial a\wedge  ar\partial a; \end{equation} this is a positive $(1,1)$-form, because $ ar\partial a=\overline{\partial a}$ for real $a$. If $B$ is a ball, a collar of $\partial B$ is an open neighborhood of the sphere $\partial B$. Several proofs below make bounded plurisubharmonic functions coincide on a common collar as follows. Let $B=B(z_0,s)$, let $U$ be an open neighborhood of $\overline B$, put $\psi =|z-z_0|^2-s^2$, and let $N\ge 1$ and $\delta >0$. If $v$ is plurisubharmonic on $U$ and $-N\le v\le -1$ there, then the function 9.8  egin{equation} \label{eq:bt-collar-modification} \max \{ v,A\psi \} ,\qquad A=N/\delta , \end{equation} is plurisubharmonic on $U$ (proposition 8.2.5 ). It equals $v$ on $\{ \psi <-\delta \} $, where $A\psi <-N$, and it equals $A\psi $ on the collar $\{ z\in U:\psi (z)>-1/A\} $ of $\partial B$, where $A\psi >-1$; on $B$, where $\psi <0$, it takes values in $[-N,0)$. Since $t\mapsto \max \{ t,A\psi (z)\} $ is increasing and continuous, the modification preserves inequalities between functions, monotone sequences, and convergence at each point. Lemma 9.2.1 . Let $B$ be a ball and $R$ a closed positive current of bidimension $(1,1)$ on a neighborhood of $\overline B$. Let $a$ be a smooth function and $b$ a bounded plurisubharmonic function on a neighborhood of $\overline B$, and suppose that $a=b$ on a collar of $\partial B$. Then \[  \int _ B a\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}b\wedge R=\int _ B b\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R.  \] In particular one may take $R=S\wedge \gamma $, where $S$ is a closed positive current, $\gamma $ is a smooth closed form of pure bidegree, and $S\wedge \gamma $ is positive of bidimension $(1,1)$. Proof. Both integrals are finite, since $a$ and $b$ are bounded on $B$ and $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R$ and $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}b\wedge R$ are measures of finite total variation near $\overline B$. The function $h=b-a$ is bounded and Borel, and it vanishes on a collar $V$ of $\partial B$; hence its restriction to $B$ vanishes outside the compact set $K_0=\overline B\setminus V\subset B$. On $B$ we have $bR=aR+hR$, and since $R$ is closed, $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(aR)=\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R$ by ( 9.1 ); thus \[  \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}b\wedge R=\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R+\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(hR)\quad 	ext{on }B,  \] where $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(hR)$ is a signed measure with support in $K_0$. Choose $\chi \in C^\infty _ c(B)$ equal to $1$ on a neighborhood of $K_0$. By the definition of $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}$ on currents, \[  \int _ Ba\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(hR)=\int \chi a\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(hR)=\int h\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(\chi a)\wedge R=\int _ Bh\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R.  \] Consequently \[  \int _ Ba\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}b\wedge R=\int _ Ba\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R+\int _ Bh\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R=\int _ Bb\, \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}a\wedge R.  \] The last assertion holds because $S\wedge \gamma $ is then closed, by the Leibniz rule, and positive of bidimension $(1,1)$; moreover $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}b\wedge (S\wedge \gamma )=(\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}b\wedge S)\wedge \gamma $ by proposition 9.1.2 (4). $\square$ Lemma 9.2.2 . Let $\mu _ j	o \mu $ weakly, where $\mu _ j$ and $\mu $ are positive Radon measures on $\Omega $, and let $f_ j$ be real-valued upper semicontinuous functions on $\Omega $ which decrease to a locally bounded function $f$. If $f_{j_ k}\mu _{j_ k}	o 
