otin \overline{U'_ j}$ do not occur near $x_0$. If $x_0\in \partial U'_ j$, then $\chi _ j=0$ near $x_0$, because $\chi _ j$ has compact support in $U'_ j$; so near $x_0$ the $j$th term, where it is defined, equals $v_ j\leq v_ i+a=v_ i+a\chi _ i$, since $\chi _ i=1$ on the neighborhood $U_ i$ of $x_0$. Such a term never exceeds the $i$th term and can be omitted. Hence on a small neighborhood of $x_0$, $v$ is the maximum of $v_ j+a\chi _ j$ over the fixed set of indices $j$ with $x_0\in U'_ j$. Each of these functions satisfies $	heta +aC_\chi \omega +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(v_ j+a\chi _ j)\geq 0$. Adding a local potential of the closed form $	heta +aC_\chi \omega $ on a coordinate ball about $x_0$ (lemma 13.1.2 ), which makes these functions plurisubharmonic by lemma 13.1.4 (2), and applying proposition 8.2.5 , we obtain that $v$ is quasi-plurisubharmonic near $x_0$ with $	heta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}v\geq -aC_\chi \omega $. On $U_ j$ the $j$th term is $v_ j+a\geq v_ j$, and every term with index $l$ is at most $v_ l+a\leq v_ j+2a$. This proves the two bounds, also at points of the pole sets. $\square$ Now fix a smooth closed real $(1,1)$-form $	heta $ on $X$. By lemma 13.1.2 we can choose smooth real functions $h_ j$ on $B_ j^+$ with $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h_ j=	heta $. Let $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(X,	heta )$ and let $m\geq 1$ be an integer. Then $\varphi +h_ j$ is plurisubharmonic on $B_ j$ by lemma 13.1.4 (2), and we let $K_{j,m}$ be the Bergman kernel of section 13.2 on the ball $B_ j$ for the weight $m(\varphi +h_ j)$ and the volume form $\omega ^ n/n!$: \[  K_{j,m}(x)=\sup \Bigl \{ |F(x)|^2:F\in \mathcal O(B_ j),\  \int _{B_ j}|F|^2\mathrm e^{-m(\varphi +h_ j)} rac{\omega ^ n}{n!}\leq 1\Bigr \} .  \] We put \[  a_{j,m}=\mathrm e^{-mh_ j}K_{j,m},\qquad v_{j,m}=m^{-1}\log a_{j,m}=m^{-1}\log K_{j,m}-h_ j.  \] The closure of $B_ j$ lies in a chart, so in the coordinates of $B_ j$ the density of $\omega ^ n/n!$ with respect to Lebesgue measure is bounded above and below by positive constants on $B_ j$. Hence theorem 13.2.3 , in the version with the measure $\varrho \, \mathrm dV=\omega ^ n/n!$, applies on $B_ j$: $m^{-1}\log K_{j,m}$ is plurisubharmonic, and $v_{j,m}\in \mathrm{PSH}(B_ j,	heta )$. Lemma 13.3.2 . There is a constant $C_ B\geq 0$, depending only on the fixed geometry and in particular independent of $	heta $, $\varphi $ and $m$, such that \[  a_{j,m}\leq \mathrm e^{C_ B}a_{l,m}\qquad 	ext{on }\overline{U'_ j}\cap \overline{U'_ l}  \] for all $j,l$. In particular $a_{j,m}$ and $a_{l,m}$ have the same zeros on $\overline{U'_ j}\cap \overline{U'_ l}$, and $|v_{j,m}-v_{l,m}|\leq C_ B/m$ there outside these common zeros. Proof. Fix $j,l$ with $\overline{U'_ j}\cap \overline{U'_ l}
eq \varnothing $, and work in the coordinates of the ball $\mathcal B_{jl}$. By lemma 13.1.2 choose a smooth function $g$ on $\mathcal B_{jl}$ with $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}g=	heta $. The functions $h_ j-g$ and $h_ l-g$ are pluriharmonic on the coordinate balls $B_ j^+$ and $B_ l^+$, so by the same lemma $h_ j-g=\operatorname{Re}b_ j$ and $h_ l-g=\operatorname{Re}b_ l$ with $b_ j,b_ l$ holomorphic on these balls (the constants are absorbed into $b_ j,b_ l$). Put $u=\varphi +g$, a plurisubharmonic function on $\mathcal B_{jl}$. Since $|\mathrm e^{-mb_ j/2}|^2=\mathrm e^{-m\operatorname{Re}b_ j}$, multiplication by $\mathrm e^{-mb_ j/2}$ is an isometry from the weighted space defining $K_{j,m}$ onto the space of holomorphic functions on $B_ j$ with weight $mu$ and volume form $\omega ^ n/n!$. If $K^{mu}_{B_ j}$ denotes the Bergman kernel of the latter space, then $K^{mu}_{B_ j}=\mathrm e^{-m\operatorname{Re}b_ j}K_{j,m}$, and therefore \[  a_{j,m}=\mathrm e^{-mg}K^{mu}_{B_ j},\qquad a_{l,m}=\mathrm e^{-mg}K^{mu}_{B_ l}.  \] Hence it suffices to prove $K^{mu}_{B_ j}\leq C_0^2K^{mu}_{B_ l}$ on $\overline{U'_ j}\cap \overline{U'_ l}$ with $C_0\geq 1$ depending only on the fixed geometry. Choose a smooth function $ho \colon X	o [0,1]$ with compact support in $B_ j\cap B_ l$, equal to one on a neighborhood of the compact set $\overline{U'_ j}\cap \overline{U'_ l}$ (proposition 7.1.3 ); this choice belongs to the fixed geometry. Let $r>0$ be the distance, in the coordinates of $\mathcal B_{jl}$, from $\overline{U'_ j}\cap \overline{U'_ l}$ to the support of $ ar\partial ho $, and let $c>0$ and $C''$ be lower and upper bounds for the density of $\omega ^ n/n!$ with respect to Lebesgue measure on $B_ j\cup B_ l$ in these coordinates. The ball $B_ l$ is pseudoconvex also in the coordinates of $\mathcal B_{jl}$, since a biholomorphism transports a continuous plurisubharmonic exhaustion function (proposition 8.2.3 ). To compare the kernels at a point $x\in \overline{U'_ j}\cap