Theorem A.7.6 . Let $X$ be a compact complex space. Every prime divisor over $X$ is represented on a smooth model obtained from $X$ by a finite sequence of blowups with smooth centers. Its representative may also be chosen smooth. Proof. After reducing to the case of a manifold, we blow up the image of the divisor in $X$ as long as it has codimension at least two; each step lowers the order of vanishing along the divisor of the Jacobian determinant of the model. Step 1: reduction to a manifold. Let $\widehat X	o X$ be a resolution given by theorem A.5.4 , a finite sequence of blowups with smooth centers, since $X$ is compact. Let the prime divisor be represented by $(M_0,F_0)$, and let $M$ be a common smooth model of $M_0$ and $\widehat X$, with $E\subset M$ the strict transform of $F_0$ (proposition A.7.3 ); then $(M,E)$ represents the same divisor, as we see on the common smooth model $M$. A finite sequence of blowups with smooth centers over $\widehat X$, composed with $\widehat X	o X$, is such a sequence over $X$, and smooth models of $\widehat X$ are smooth models of $X$, by corollary A.6.3 . Hence we may assume that $X$ is a compact complex manifold, and we are given a proper bimeromorphic map $f\colon M	o X$ from a compact manifold and an irreducible hypersurface $E\subset M$. Since $E$ is irreducible, hence connected, it lies in a connected component $M_ E$ of $M$, and $f(M_ E)$ lies in a connected component $X_ E$ of $X$. These components have the same dimension $n$, because $M_ E$ contains a nonempty open set mapped isomorphically onto an open subset of $X$ (lemma A.3.1 ); moreover $n\geq 1$ and $\dim E=n-1$, since $E$ is a nonempty hypersurface. If $n=1$, the center $f(E)$ considered below is a point of $X_ E$, hence a hypersurface. Step 2: the order of the Jacobian. The Jacobian determinant $J_ f$ of $f$ in local coordinates is holomorphic and determined up to multiplication by holomorphic units. It does not vanish identically near any point, because $f$ is an isomorphism over a dense open subset of $X$ whose inverse image is dense in $M$ (lemma A.3.1 ). Its order of vanishing $\operatorname {ord}_ EJ_ f$ along $E$ is the largest $k$ such that $J_ f$ is divisible by the $k$th power of a local equation of $E$ near points of $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$; definition 12.6.3 uses the same notion of order along a hypersurface. It is a finite nonnegative integer $k$, since a function divisible by all powers of a local coordinate $y$ has vanishing Taylor coefficients in $y$ and vanishes near the point. It does not depend on the point of $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$, which is a connected manifold by corollary A.2.14 : for each integer $j$, the set of points of $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$ at which the order is at least $j$ is open, and it is closed by the identity theorem on $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$, since a holomorphic function vanishing on $E$ near a regular point is divisible by a local equation of $E$. For composable maps the chain rule gives A.14  egin{equation} \label{eq:divisor-jacobian-order-chain} \operatorname {ord}_ E J_{g\circ h} =\operatorname {ord}_ E J_ h+ \operatorname {ord}_ E(J_ g\circ h), \end{equation} because orders of vanishing along $E$ add under multiplication. Replacing $M$ by a common smooth model $h\colon M'	o M$ and $E$ by its strict transform $E'$ does not change $k$: by proposition A.7.3 , $h$ is an isomorphism near generic points of $E'$, so $\operatorname {ord}_{E'}J_ h=0$ and $\operatorname {ord}_{E'}(J_ f\circ h)=k$. Step 3: one principalization lowers the order. The center $Z=f(E)$ is an analytic subset of $X$ by theorem A.6.2 , of dimension at most $\dim E=n-1$ by lemma A.2.12 (3). It is irreducible: if $Z=Z_1\cup Z_2$ with analytic subsets $Z_1,Z_2$, then the irreducible set $E$ is the union of its analytic subsets $E\cap f^{-1}(Z_1)$ and $E\cap f^{-1}(Z_2)$, so it lies in one of them, and $Z$ equals $Z_1$ or $Z_2$. The same argument shows that images of irreducible analytic sets under proper holomorphic maps are irreducible. Suppose first that $Z$ is a hypersurface, and let $S\subset X$ be as in proposition A.7.3 for $f$. The strict transform $Z^ M$ of $Z$ in $M$ is an irreducible hypersurface containing $f^{-1}(Z\setminus S)$. The set $E\cap f^{-1}(Z\setminus S)$ is open in $E$, and it is nonempty, because $f(E)=Z$ is not contained in $S$, which has codimension at least two. So $E\cap Z^ M$ is an analytic subset of $E$ with interior points in $E$, and $E\subset Z^ M$ by corollary A.2.14 (2); then $E=Z^ M$ by corollary A.2.14 (3). Hence $(X,Z)$ represents the given divisor, as we see on the common smooth model $M$. Suppose now that $Z$ has codimension at least two. Its ideal $\mathcal I_ Z$ is coherent by theorem A.2.8 , and it is not identically zero on any component of $X$, since $Z$ is nowhere dense. Let $\sigma =\sigma _1\circ \cdots \circ \sigma _ t\colon X'	o X$ be the finite sequence of blowups with centers $C_ i\subset X_{i-1}$ and transformed ideals $\mathfrak a_ i$, $\mathfrak a_0=\mathcal I_ Z$, given by theorem A.5.5 ; it is proper and bimeromorphic by corollary A.6.3 , so $X'$ is a smooth model of $X$. Let $h\colon M'	o M$ and $f'\colon M'	o X'$ be the maps from a common smooth model of $M$ and $X'$, so that $f\circ h=\sigma \circ f'$, let $E'$ be the strict transform of $E$, let $ho _ i\colon M'	o X_ i$ be the induced maps, and let $Z_ i=ho _ i(E')$; thus $Z_0=Z$. Let $r\geq 2$ be the codimension of $Z$, which is pure by corollary A.2.14 (1). We claim that for some $i$ the center $C_ i$ contains $Z_{i-1}$, and that the connected component of $C_ i$ containing $Z_{i-1}$ has codimension $r$. The sets $Z_ j$ are irreducible, as images of the irreducible set $E'$, and $\sigma _{j+1}(Z_{j+1})=Z_ j$. Put $	au _ j=\sigma _1\circ \cdots \circ \sigma _ j$. Suppose that $Z_{l-1}
